# What the borg manages, so that a drop is noticed.
#
# The nightly cannot use pass and fail for this. Every run dies -- that is what
# unaided play at these levels looks like -- and a run that hits the clock has
# not failed either, so the job goes red only for a crash or a wedge. Without
# this file best depth could halve overnight and the job would stay green,
# which is exactly the regression the nightly exists to catch.
#
# `scripts/borg-progress -b tests/borg/BASELINE` compares a sweep against these
# and fails if a number has dropped materially. Same shape as
# tests/saves/EXPECTED-FAILURES: a committed statement of what is true now,
# which somebody has to change on purpose.
#
# === Updating it when a number legitimately improves ===
#
# Take the sweep the nightly takes, at the commit you are about to push:
#
#     scripts/borg-progress -n 2000000 -m 10 \
#         -c Warrior,Mage,Priest,Warrior-Mage 1 7 13
#
# Copy its three summary lines into the values below, update the notes saying
# which run reached each best, and say in the commit message why the number
# moved. That last part is the point of the file: a baseline that rises without
# an explanation is a baseline nobody trusts to fall.
#
# Do not update it to make a red night go green. If a number dropped and you do
# not know why, that is the finding.
#
# === What these numbers depend on ===
#
# The class list, the seeds, and the turn budget -- all three are in the
# workflow and all three are part of what is being measured. Changing any of
# them invalidates the file, so change them and re-baseline in the same commit.
#
# The runs are exact: the same seed on the same build gives the same turn count
# every time, verified three times over. So nothing here is noise. But any
# change to the game *data* reshuffles the random stream and moves which seed
# happens to do well -- which is why the thresholds in borg-progress tolerate a
# couple of levels rather than demanding the figures never move at all.

